                  "pages": "หน้า 2–7",
                  "text": "▪ Antacid pH > 4 → to neutralize acid, inactivate pepsin, bind\nbile salts, enhance mucosal defense\n▪ Effect: symptom relief and ulcer healing (poor)\n▪ No longer used as monotherapy → low efficacy,\ninconvenience, ADRs\n▪ Take pc. is better than ac. >> longer neutralize 3-4 hours\nFeature NaHCO3 CaCO3 MgOH2 AlOH3\nOnset Rapid Intermediate Rapid Slow\nDuration Short Moderate Moderate Moderate\nAlkalosis Yes ? No No\nStool --- Constipation Diarrhea Constipation\n• ▪ Antacids with high acid neutralizing capacity (ANC) are more\neffective\n▪ Sodium bicarbonate, Calcium carbonate → greatest\nANC BUT unacceptable SE → alkalosis\n▪ Most widely used: Magnesium and Aluminum hydroxide\n▪ Sodium bicarbonate is readily absorbed while CaCO 3,\nAluminum hydroxide and Magnesium hydroxide are poorly\nabsorbed.\n• ▪ Dosage form\n▪ Tablet\n▪ Mg(OH)2 200-400 mg + Al(OH)3 200-600 mg\n▪ +/- Kaolin 50 mg or Simeticone 40-125 mg\n▪ Regimen 1-2 tab tid-qid pc\n▪ Suspension\n▪ Mg(OH)2 103-500 mg/tsp + Al(OH)3 165-650 mg/tsp\n▪ +/- Simeticone 20-40 mg/tsp\n▪ Regimen 30 ml tid-qid pc\n• ▪ Dosage form\n▪ Tablet, Suspension (per 10 ml)\n▪ NaHCO3 95-300 mg\n▪ CaCO3 70-360 mg\n▪ Sodium alginate 250-500 mg\n• ▪ ADRs\n▪ GI side effect:\n▪ Magnesium → diarrhea\n▪ Aluminum and calcium → constipation\n▪ Aluminum and magnesium accumulation in patient with\nrenal disease\n▪ Systemic alkalosis → Sodium bicarbonate\n▪ Rebound hyperacidity and milk alkali syndrome →\nCaCO3\n• ▪ Drug interaction\n▪ Calcium, Aluminum and Magnesium : decrease\nabsorption of tetracycline, fluoroquinolone, iron,\nwarfarin, digoxin, phenytoin, ketoconazole\n▪ Aluminum forms insoluble salts with phosphorus dietary\n▪ Take antacid separately → at least 2 hours\n▪ Warning\n▪ Sodium content: patients with CHF\n▪ Magnesium and aluminum content: patients with renal\nimpairment (ClCr < 30 ml/min)"
