                  "pages": "หน้า 4–28",
                  "text": "Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Headache Disorders in Adults, 2012.\n• Timeline of migraine attacks\nMigraine Buddy, 2015, Blau J N Lancet, 1992\n• Pathophysiology (Vascular theory)\nVascularity abnormalities during MG.\n• Trigger factors\n■ Physical exertion\n■ Hormone changes\n■ Diet\n■ Stress and anxiety\n■ Sleep deprivation or excess\n■ Other environment factors\nAdapt form: Pharmacotherapy A Pathophysiologic Approach 13rd edition, 2024.\n• Severity of acute migraine\n■ Mild\n– Occasional throbbing headache and no major\nfunctional impairment\n■ Moderate\n– Some impairment of functioning and nausea\n■ Severe\n– More than three migraines in a month, significant\nfunctional impairment, and marked nausea and\nvomiting.\n• Type of migraine\n■ Episodic migraine (acute migraine)\n– Migraine with aura\n– Migraine without aura\n■ Chronic migraine\n• แนวทางเวชปฏิบัติการวินิจฉัยและการรักษาปวดศีรษะไมเกรน, 2565.\n• Acute treatment of migraine\n■ Rapid and consistent freedom from pain and associated\nsymptoms without recurrence\n■ Restored ability to function\n■ Minimal need for repeat dosing or rescue medications\n■ Optimal self-care and reduced subsequent use of\nresources\n■ Minimal or no AEs\nHeadache 2021;61:1021–1039.\n• Acute treatment of migraine\nAdapt from: Headache 2021;61:1021–1039.\nEstablished efficacy Probably effective\nMigraine-specific\n• Triptans\n• Gepants\n• Lasmiditan\n• Ergotamine\nNonspecific\n• NSAIDs: aspirin, celecoxib oral\nsolution, diclofenac, ibuprofen,\nnaproxen\n• Combination analgesic:\nacetaminophen + aspirin + caffeine\n• NSAIDs: flurbiprofen, ketoprofen, IV/IM\nketorolac\n• IV magnesium\n• Antiemetics: chlorpromazine,\nmetoclopramide, promethazine\n• Acute treatment of migraine\n■ NSAIDs (including aspirin), nonopioid analgesics,\nacetaminophen, or caffeinated analgesic combinations\n(eg, aspirin + acetaminophen + caffeine) for mild -to-\nmoderate attacks.\n■ Migraine-specific (triptans, dihydroergotamine[DHE],\nsmall-molecule CGRP receptor antagonists [ gepants],\nselective serotonin ( 5-HT1F) receptor agonist [ ditan]) for\nmoderate or severe attacks and mild -to-moderate\nattacks that respond poorly to nonspecific therapy.\nHeadache 2021;61:1021–1039.\n• Medication used for acute migraine\nแนวทางเวชปฏิบัติการวินิจฉัยและการรักษาปวดศีรษะไมเกรน, 2565.\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Triptans\nJ Headache Pain (2001) 2:3–11\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Triptans (activated 5-HT1B,1D receptor)\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Triptans\n– Sumatriptan: 50-100 mg SD, repeated dose after 2\nhours. (Maximum dose; 200 mg/day)\n– Eletriptan: 20-40 mg SD, repeated dose after 2\nhours. (Maximum dose; 80 mg/day)\nLimit use to < 10 days per month to avoid MOH\nDo not use within 24 hours of an ergotamine preparation or difference triptan\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Dihydroergotamine (DHE)\n– Nasal spray or subcutaneous/intramuscular\ninjection may be considered for patients who do not\nrespond well to triptans.\n■ Ergotamine (activated 5-HT1B,1D)\n– May be helpful for selected patients where triptans\nare not an option.\n■ Because it is a vasoconstrictor, it should not be used in\npatients with cerebrovascular or cardiovascular disease.\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Ergotamine (side effects)\n– Nausea and vomiting (activated D2 receptor)\n– High blood pressure (activated alpha1 receptor)\n– Numbness and ergotism (activated alpha1 receptor\nand 5-HT receptor)\n– Palpitation\n– Serotonin syndrome (with MOAI, SSRI, TCA)\n■ Metabolism via CYP3A4\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Ergotism\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Ergotism management\n– Stop ergotamine and CYP3A4 inhibitors\n– Cyanosis or peripheral ischemia\n■ Sodium nitroprussside 0.3 mcg/kg/min (and slow\ntitrate dose)\n■ Nifedipine 10 mg q 8 hrs\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Gepants\nClin Transl Sci. 2024;17:e13707.\n• Medication used for acute migraine\n■ Gepants\n– calcitonin gene-related peptide receptor antagonist\nClin Transl Sci. 2024;17:e13707."
