              "title": "H2 receptor antagonists",
              "pages": "หน้า 8–11",
              "text": "▪ Cimetidine, Ranitidine, Famotidine\n▪ MOA : competitively and reversibly bind to H2 receptors on\nparietal cells\n• ▪ ADRs\n▪ Most common: GI upset, diarrhea, drowsiness,\nheadache\n▪ Other: dizziness, confusion, (elderly: cimetidine → rash\nand sweating)\n▪ Gynecomastia: impotence can occur in men with\nprolonged or high dose of “cimetidine” ( antiandrogenic\neffect)\n▪ Thrombocytopenia → reversible\n▪ Ranitidine → less side effect\n▪ Famotidine, nizatidine → NO antiandrogenic effect\n• ▪ Drug interaction\n▪ Cimetidine: interact with drugs metabolized by CYP 3A4\nEx. Theophylline, phenytoin, warfarin, beta -blockers,\nlidocaine, diazepam\n▪ Ranitidine: Less interact with hepatic CYP system,\ndecrease renal clearance of procainamide, increase\neffect of glipizide.\n▪ Famotidine and nizatidine: less affect to hepatic\nclearance and other drugs\n▪ Antacid, metoclopamide: decrease bioavailability of\ncimetidine separate admin time at least 2 hours\n• Basic heterocyclic\ngroup\nFlexible chain\n(4 atom is optimal)\nPolar group"
