            {
              "title": "Mechanism of action",
              "pages": "หน้า 3",
              "text": "PPIs are weak base pro -drugs, that once in the acidic extracellular environment surrounding\ntumors, can be protonated, thereby reducing their ability to cross the membrane of cells. PPIs\nthen bind irreversibly to proton pumps, dramatically inhibiting their activity, leading to inhibition\nof proton translocation across the plasma membrane, which in turn, induces alkalization of the\ntumor microenvironment.\nMetabolites 2018, 8, 2;doi:10.3390/metabo8010002"
            },
            {
              "title": "PPIs Chemistry",
              "pages": "หน้า 4",
              "text": "American Journal of Pharmaceutical Education 2006; 70 (5) Article 101."
            },
            {
              "title": "Chemical structure of PPIs",
              "pages": "หน้า 6",
              "text": "Walsh et al. Journal of Experimental & Clinical Cancer Research (2015) 34:93"
            },
            {
              "title": "PPIs action",
              "pages": "หน้า 7–9",
              "text": "Walsh et al. Journal of Experimental & Clinical Cancer Research (2015) 34:93\nDrug-enzyme complex\n• Nature Reviews Drug Discovery volume 2, pages 132–139 (2003)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Omeprazole VS Esomeprazole",
              "pages": "หน้า 10–12",
              "text": "Synthesis of omeprazole and esomeprazole. The large -scale production of esomeprazole is achieved by\nasymmetric oxidation of the same sulphide intermediate as is used in the production of omeprazole, which\ngives a 94% enantiomeric excess (ee). This is increased to 100% by preparing a magnesium salt of of\nesomeprazole and then performing a crystallization.\nNature Reviews Drug Discovery volume 2, pages 132–139 (2003)\n• Effects of racemic omeprazole and its enantiomers. a | Drug plasma concentrations and b\n| inhibition of pentagastrin-stimulated gastric acid secretion in healthy subjects (n= 4)\nafter oral administration of 15 mg of R-omeprazole,omeprazole and esomeprazole at time\nNature Reviews Drug Discovery volume 2, pages 132–139 (2003)\n• Optimal dosing of esomeprazole. Mean plasma concentration -time profiles on day 5 after\ndaily doses of esomeprazole (40 and 20 mg) and omeprazole (20 mg) in 36 patients with\ngastroesophageal reflux disease 47. 40 mg of esomeprazole taken orally showed much\nhigher and more prolonged plasma concentration curves than 20 mg esomeprazole.\nNature Reviews Drug Discovery volume 2, pages 132–139 (2003)"
            }
