            {
              "title": "Algorithm for treating women with abnormal",
              "pages": "หน้า 5",
              "text": "vaginal discharge\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge"
            },
            {
              "title": "Bacterial vaginosis",
              "pages": "หน้า 6–11",
              "text": "■ BV is not considered as an STI because it is caused by an\nimbalance of bacterial community in vagina. There is an\nincrease of anaerobic bacteria replacing Lactobacilli spp.\n– Anaerobic bacteria (Prevotella sp., Mobiluncus sp.)\n– Gardnerella vaginalis\n– Mycoplasma hominis\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Signs and symptoms\nBV is mostly asymptomatic. Those who have symptoms often\npresent with whitish or grayish vaginal discharge or with fishy -odor\ndischarge. The smell becomes striking during sexual intercourse and\naround menstrual period\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Risk factors\n– Disturbance of vaginal ecosystem includes vaginal douching,\nexcessive genital cleansing, prolonged vaginal bleeding or\nfollowing any kinds of vaginal infection\n– Multiple sex partners\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Diagnosing BV by Amsel criteria requires the presence of 3\nout of 4 criteria;\n– Fishy-odor discharge either before or after adding 10% KOH\n(whiff test)\n– Homogeneous creamy grayish-white discharge,\n– Presence of clue cells\n– Vaginal pH is > 4.5,\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Recommended treatment of bacterial vaginosis\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Metronidazole (ยับยั้ง CYP2C9)\n– ผลข้างเคียง คลื่นไส้ อาเจียน ท้องผูก ลิ้น /ปากอักเสบ ชัก (high doses)\nleucopenia, thrombocytopenia, metallic taste\n– คําแนะนํา หลีกเลี่ยงเครื่องดื่มที่มีแอลกอฮอล์ระหว่างใช้ยาและ 24 ชม. หลัง\nกินยา กินยาติดต่อกันจนหมด\n■ Tinidazole\n– ผลข้างเคียง คลื่นไส้ อาเจียน ปัสสาวะสีเข้ม , hypersensitivity, metallic\ntaste\n– คําแนะนํา หลีกเลี่ยงเครื่องดื่มที่มีแอลกอฮอล์ระหว่างใช้ยาและ 72 ชม. หลัง\nกินยา กินยาติดต่อกันจนหมด"
            },
            {
              "title": "Disulfiram like reaction",
              "pages": "หน้า 12",
              "text": "Nitroimidazole ring"
            },
            {
              "title": "Vaginal trichomoniasis",
              "pages": "หน้า 14–19",
              "text": "■ Vaginal trichomoniasis is an STI caused by Trichomonas\nvaginalis which has an oval shape and is sized slightly\nlarger than a leukocyte. It has a jerky movement due to\nhaving five flagella.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Signs and symptoms\n– Vaginal trichomoniasis can be asymptomatic.\n– Patients with symptoms often present with yellow or green\nfrothy vaginal discharge and itching in the vagina.\n– Some may have fishy-odor discharge and dysuria.\n– The pelvic examination may reveal inflamed vaginal mucosa\nand friable cervix with bleeding beneath the mucosa\n(strawberry cervix).\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Risk factors\n– Vaginal trichomoniasis is a disease that is transmitted only\nfrom person to person through sexual intercourse.\n– Risk factors are therefore related to unprotected sex with\npeople with the disease.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Diagnosis\n– The simplest bed -side diagnostic tool is wet preparation.\nHowever, the sensitivity is only 40 -70% and its accuracy is\ngreatly reduced if examination is performed later than an hour\nof specimen collection.\n– Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is an alternative option.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Recommended treatment of Vaginal trichomoniasis\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge"
            },
            {
              "title": "Vaginal candidiasis",
              "pages": "หน้า 20–28",
              "text": "■ Vaginal candidiasis (VC) is the leading cause of abnormal\nvaginal discharge.\n■ The most common pathogen is Candida albicans, at about\n80-90%, followed by C. glabrata, C. tropicalis, C. krusei, etc.\n■ As C. albicans is a normal flora that can live in the vagina.\nVC is not classified as an STI.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Signs and symptoms\n– The symptoms of VC are itching inside or outside the vagina\nand abnormal vaginal discharge such as increased amount or\ncurd-like appearance.\n– In case of severe symptoms, external genital area is involved\ncausing erythema, swelling, painful skin, abrasions, or\ndyspareunia.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Risk factors\n– Those factors are often related to blood sugar levels, including\npoorly controlled diabetes, pregnancy, steroid use etc.\n– Long-term use of antibiotics\n– Receiving additional hormones (birth control pills containing\nestrogen, hormone replacement therapy in menopause)\n– Additional factors related to stress or mood swings, iron\ndeficiency anemia and excessive cleaning of the genitals have\nalso been reported\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Diagnosis\n– Examination of high vaginal swab under a microscope is the\nmost practical method.\n– For fungal culture, it is reserved for recurrent VC as it can be\nused to detect drug-resistant strains.\n– Only clinical diagnosis by history or naked -eye diagnosis is not\naccepted.\n– At present, screening for other STIs among women with VC is\nnot recommended\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Classification of vulvovaginal candidiasis (VVC)\nUncomplicated VVC\n– Sporadic or infrequent VVC and\n– Mild-to-moderate VVC and\n– Likely to be Candida albicans and\n– Nonimmunocompromised women\nComplicated VVC\n– Recurrent VVC or\n– Severe VVC or\n– Non-albicans candidiasis or\n– Women with diabetes, immunocompromising conditions (e.g., HIV infection), underlying\nimmunodeficiency, or immunosuppressive therapy (e.g., corticosteroids)\nAbbreviation: HIV, human immunodeficiency virus.\nCDC 2021\n• ■ Recommended treatment of acute vaginal candidiasis.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Recommended treatment of recurrent vaginal candidiasis.\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge\n• ■ Treatment of severe vulvovaginal candidiasis\n– Severe VVC (i.e., extensive vulvar erythema, edema, excoriation,\nand fissure formation) is associated with lower clinical\nresponse rates among patients treated with short courses of\ntopical or oral therapy.\n– Either 7–14 days of topical azole or 150 mg of fluconazole in\ntwo sequential oral doses (second dose 72 hours after initial\ndose) is recommended.\nCDC 2021"
            },
            {
              "title": "Summary",
              "pages": "หน้า 29",
              "text": "■ Characteristics of vaginal discharge by various conditions\nThe Thai 2022 Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment  Guideline: Abnormal vaginal discharge"
            }
