              "title": "Therapeutic options",
              "pages": "หน้า 20–48",
              "text": "Relative glucocorticoid receptor affinity of intranasal corticosteroids\nDerendorf H, Meltzer EO. Allergy 2008: 63: 1292–1300.\n• Systemic absorption rates of intranasal corticosteroids\nDerendorf H, Meltzer EO. Allergy 2008: 63: 1292–1300.\n• ยา corticosteroid พ่นจมูก อายุต่่าสุดในเด็กที่แนะน่าให้ใช้ยา\nBeclomethasone dipropionate\nBudesonide\nTriamcinolone acetonide\nFluticasone propionate\nMomethasone furoate\nFluticasone furoate\n6 ปี\n6 ปี\n2 ปี\n4 ปี\n2 ปี\n2 ปี\nบุษบา จินดาวิจักษณ์ และ ธนรัตน์ สรวลเสน่ห์. Pharmacotherapy of interesting common disease. ประชาชน จากัด. กรุงเทพฯ: 2556.\n• Fluticasone furoate Momethasone furoate\nFluticasone propionate Budesonide\n• Oral antihistamine\n■ First line treatment for mild allergic rhinitis\n■ 2nd generation oral H1-antihistamines are\npreferred for their favorable efficacy/safety ratio\nand pharmacokinetics\n■ 2nd generation medications can be used once\ndaily\n■ Rapidly effective (less than 1 hour) on nasal and\nocular symptoms\n■ Poorly effective on nasal congestion\n• Oral antihistamine\nGolan DE, Tashjian AH, Armstrong EJ and Armstrong AW. Principles of pharmacology:\nThe Pathophysiologic Basis of Drug Therapy. 3rd ed. Baltimore: Lippincott Williams & Wilkins; 2012.\n• Antihistamine Sedative effects Antihistamine activity Anticholinergic activity Antiemetic effects\nFirst generation (nonselective)\nAllkylamine\nBrompheniramine + +++ ++ NA\nChlorpheniramine + ++ ++ NA\nEthanolamine\nDiphenhydramine +++ + to ++ +++ ++ to +++\nPhenothiazine\nPromethazine +++ +++ +++ ++++\nPiperazine\nHydroxyzine +++ ++ to +++ ++ +++\n++++ Very high\n+++ High\n++ Moderate\n+ Low\n+/- Low to None\nNA Not available\nAdapt form: Drug fact and Comparisons. 2013.\n• Antihistamine Sedative effects Antihistamine activity Anticholinergic activity Antiemetic effects\nSecond generation\nPiperazine\nCetirizine + ++ to +++ +/- NA\nLevocetirizine NA NA NA NA\nPiperidine\nLoratadine +/- +++ +/- NA\nDesloratadine +/- NA +/- NA\nFexofenadine +/- NA +/- NA\n++++ Very high\n+++ High\n++ Moderate\n+ Low\n+/- Low to None\nNA Not available\nAdapt form: Drug fact and Comparisons. 2013.\n• Golan DE, Tashjian AH, Armstrong EJ and Armstrong AW. Principles of pharmacology:\nThe Pathophysiologic Basis of Drug Therapy. 3rd ed. Baltimore: Lippincott Williams & Wilkins; 2012.\nRelative potential for causing drowsiness in patients receiving\nsecond-generation H1 antihistamines.\n• Local antihistamine (nasal and ocular)\n■ Blockage of H1 receptor\n■ Rapidly effective (< 30 mins) on nasal or ocular\nsymptoms\n■ Not available in Thailand\n• Oral decongestants\n■ Combined with oral antihistamine\n■ Efficacy less than topical decongestants\n■ Systemic side effects: agitation, tachycardia,\npalpitation, headache, dizziness\n■ Avoid in glaucoma, BPH, thyrotoxicosis,\ncardiovascular disease, psychiatric disorders,\npregnancy\n■ Not recommend in children < 2 years\n• Oral decongestants with antihistamines\nTrade Name Compound\nNasotapp® Brompheniramine + phenylephrine\nNasolin CP® Chlorpheniramine + phenylephrine\n• Oral decongestants with antihistamine\nTrade Name Compound\nNasolin® Chlorpheniramine + pseudoephedrine\nActifed® Triprolidine + pseudoephedrine\nMaxiphed® Pseudoephedrine\n• Intranasal decongestants\n■ Sympathomimetic medication [relieve symptoms\nof nasal congestion]\n■ Act more rapidly (<10 mins) and more\neffectively than oral decongestants\n■ Greater effect on nasal obstruction than oral\ndecongestants\n• Intranasal decongestants\n■ Same side effects as oral decongestants but\nless intense\n■ Limit duration of treatment to < 5 days to avoid\nrhinitis medicamentosa\n■ Not recommend in children < 2 years\n• Intranasal decongestants\n▪ Epinephrine\n▪ Naphazoline*\n▪ Oxymethazoline*\n▪ Phenylephrine\n▪ Tetrahydrozoline\n▪ Xylomethazoline*\n• Intranasal anticholinergics\n■ Anticholinergic block almost exclusively anterior\nwatery rhinorrhea\n■ Effective in allergic and non-allergic patients\nwith rhinorrhea\n■ Long term effect\n– Urinary retention\n– Glaucoma\n– Dry nose and Epistaxis\n■ Not available in Thailand\n• Antileukotrienes\n■ Block CystLT1 receptor\n■ Equipotent to H1 receptor antagonists but with\nonset of action after 2 days\n■ Reduce nasal and systemic eosinophilia\n■ May be used for simultaneous treatment of\nallergic rhinitis and asthma\n• Antileukotrienes\nCitation: Pulmonary Pharmacology, Hilal-Dandan R, Brunton LL. Goodman and Gilman's Manual of\nPharmacology and Therapeutics, 2e; 2016.\nCopyright © 2018 McGraw-Hill Education. All rights reserved\n• Antileukotrienes\n■ Side effects\n– Headache\n– Hepatic dysfunction\n– GI side effect: abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting\n– Neuropsychiatric disorders: sedation, insomnia,\nhallucination, abnormal dream, suicidal thought\n– Churg-Strauss syndrome (rare)\n• Anti-IgE (Omalizumab)\n■ Could be considered in severe cases\nunresponsive to conventional treatment\n■ Could be an adjunct to immunotherapy in\nsevere cases\n■ Side effect: skin rash (urticaria), headache, URI,\nsinusitis, nasopharyngitis, anaphylaxis\n• Intranasal CS + Azelastine\n■ Azelastine hydrochloride\n137 mcg and fluticasone\npropionate 50 mcg\n• Anti-IgE (Omalizumab)\nCitation: Pulmonary Pharmacology, Hilal-Dandan R, Brunton LL. Goodman and Gilman's Manual of\nPharmacology and Therapeutics, 2e; 2016\nCopyright © 2018 McGraw-Hill Education. All rights reserved\n• Medication\nClasses\nSymptoms\nControlled Comments\nAntihistamines\nSystemic\nSneezing,\nrhinorrhea, itching,\nconjunctivitis\nFor seasonal allergic rhinitis, begin treatment\nbefore allergen exposure. Nonsedating agents\nshould be tried first. If ineffective or too\nexpensive for the patient, the older agents may\nbe used. For perennial allergic rhinitis, use an\nintranasal steroid as an alternative to or in\ncombination with systemic antihistamines\nOphthalmic Conjunctivitis Logical addition to nasal steroids if ocular symptoms are present\nIntranasal\nSneezing,\nrhinorrhea, nasal\npruritus\nOption for seasonal allergic rhinitis. Warn\npatients of potential drowsiness\nDecongestants\nSystemic Nasal congestion Only needed when nasal congestion is present\nTopical Nasal congestion Only needed when nasal congestion is present. Do not exceed 3-5 days\n• Medication\nClasses\nSymptoms\nControlled Comments\nIntranasal\ncorticosteroids\nSneezing, rhinorrhea,\nitching, nasal\ncongestion\nFor seasonal allergic rhinitis, an option when\ncongestion is present. Must begin therapy\nbefore allergen exposure. Excellent choice for\nperennial rhinitis\nMast cell\nstabilizers See comments\nPrevents symptoms; therefore, for seasonal\nallergic rhinitis, use before offending allergen’s\nseason starts. For perennial rhinitis,\nimprovement may not be seen for up to 1 month\nIntranasal\nanticholinergics Rhinorrhea Reserve for use when above therapies fail or cannot be tolerated\nLeukotriene\nreceptor\nantagonists\nSee comments\nWhen combined with antihistamines, more\neffective than antihistamines alone. May be\nused as monotherapy in children with asthma\nand coexisting allergic rhinitis\nCitation: Allergic Rhinitis, DiPiro JT, Talbert RL, Yee GC, Matzke GR, Wells BG, Posey L.\nPharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 10e; 2017\nCopyright © 2018 McGraw-Hill Education. All rights reserved"
